{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>For Your salvation I hope, YHWH.</b> The Ramban, of blessed memory, wrote that this verse is the prayer of Jacob, for he saw by the holy spirit that Samson was the last deliverer among the judges. And even though Samuel came after him, Samuel was a prophet and did not wage war as he did. Therefore, when Jacob saw that the deliverance of Samson was cut short with him and that he died in the collapse of the riders together, he said: 'For Your salvation I hope, YHWH' — not for the deliverance of Samson, who is likened to a serpent (נחש) and a viper (שפיפן)." "<b>And it is possible</b> to interpret that this is the prayer of Samson. For Jacob saw the end of this last judge — that he is the one who bites and brings down and rides backward — and it is he who prays 'For Your salvation I hope, YHWH,' for thus he prayed (Judges 16:28): 'Remember me, I pray, and strengthen me, I pray, just this once, O Elohim.' And even though he died among them, behold this is his deliverance, that he took vengeance upon his enemies and died with them; for in this was his desire, as it says (ibid.): 'Let me die with the Philistines.'" "<b>And know</b> that the masters of the Names derive from this verse a divine Name that is effective against adversaries. And so regarding Samson, who slew his enemies and multiplied their slain, we find written of him (ibid.): 'And he bent with might, and the house fell upon the lords and upon all the people who were in it.' Scripture did not say 'and he bent with his might (בכחו)' but rather 'and he bent with might (בכח),' meaning, with the might of the divine Name together with his own might; for he joined the might of the Name with his might and inflicted vengeance upon them. And the Name that emerges from this verse follows the order of the letters of the verse, with the words tripled, and it is well known and publicized in its vocalization. And a person must recite it in order and also in reverse, so that he may proceed on a paved path and turn his enemies backward; and from this the verse alludes: 'to him backward' (לו אחור)."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:21:38.987705600Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}